==English==

===Etymology===
Of uncertain origin. These possibilities have been suggested:
* {{etyl|ga|en}} {{m|ga|sionnachuighim||I play the fox}}.
* {{etyl|es|en}} {{m|es|chanada|}}, a shortening of {{m|es|charranada||trick, deceit}}.
* {{etyl|pfl|en}} {{m|pfl|schinägeln||to work hard}}, from the peddler's argot term {{m|pfl||Schenigelei|work}}.
* {{etyl|fr|en}} {{m|fr|manigance|ces manigances|these fraudulent schemes}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ʃəˈnænɪgən/|lang=en}},
* {{audio|shenanigan_us.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|countable}} A deceitful confidence trick, or mischief causing discomfort or annoyance.
#: ''I spotted his next '''shenanigan''' — saw it coming — and so avoided being fooled.''
# {{lb|en|rare}} {{inflection of|shenanigans||s|lang=en}}

